INFO 7721 :: Page 63 :: 740 pm Baltimore Jany 23d 1865 630 pm for CA Dana Our Agt at Benwood telegraphs at four twelve this PM that he has dispatches following troops trains this day ---- number one at five ten am with sixteen cars troops, number two at eight twenty am with twelve cars troops, number three at eight thirty am with ten cars troops baggage etc ---- number four at eleven <deletion>fifteen</deletion> <insertion>am</insertion> with fifteen cars troops etc number five at one fifteen pm with thirteen cars troops &c ---- number six at two fifteen pm with twelve cars troops etc which includes all troops arrived at Belleair up to that time ---- he adds that further arrivals are expected at five this pm ---- twenty six hundred troops have been transferred and forwarded ---- The Agt at Wheeling telegraphs that the movement is working finely and that Schofield and two hundred of his party were despatched at eleven twenty am and that the commissary arrangements are working satisfactorily J W. Garrett Prest 220 PM Pittsburgh Jany 24th 1865 1130 AM Genl Halleck. Your telegraph to Genl Meagher has been shown to me he left here yesterday. Orders have been given as directed by you. Troops have not arrived and am unable to say where they are ---- O Cross Colonel & Depy Quarter Master Genl P
INFO 8561 :: Page 83 :: 4 P. M. Ft Monroe Feby 2nd 1865 4 P. M. for Gen Schofield Washn or Annapolis . I arrived here today with despatches for you from Gen Sherman will be in Annapolis tomorrow morning (Friday) sig John F Anderson Maj & A. D. C. 710 pm 2d Ft Monroe Feb 2 1865 Capt Blodgett a Q m Annapolis ---- Please inform Gen Schofield should he arrive in annapolis tonight that I will be at Annapolis to morrow Fryday morning with important dispatches for him from Gen Sherman J. F Anderson Major & A. D. C. 7 PM 8 pm Annapolis Feb 2 1865 Gen D. H. Rucker Telegram recd . The steamer Ariel can take one thousand men & 60 officers The New York can take 1500 . To put more on would crowd them very much . Gen Meagher requests that steamers which have reported be directed to take his command G. S. Blodgett Capt. A Q M
INFO 8531 :: Page 53 :: 235 pm Fort Monroe Jany 23d 1865 2 pm for BrGen D H Rucker Q Mr Washn . I send today the El cid and Ranger . the Cassandria is just here and will be sent as soon as discharged ---- R C. Webster Col & Q Mr 245 pm Baltimore Jany 23d 1865 2pm 23d CA Dana I have your advices with Col P's reports . one Regt the twenty eighth Mich arrived yesterday am at Benwood and was at once forwarded . In consequence of the storm the wires are working badly to Wheeling and we have not advices of further arrivals at the river . We judge how ever by our information from Cincinnati and Columbus that troops must now be arriving at Benwood and loading rapidly . the weather is very bad raining sleeting and snowing I have however ordered large forces upon the track over the whole line who are working energetically to insure successful results at nine am yesterday I ordered suspension of all business except military and RR upon the telegraph lines between Wheeling Parkersburg and Balt. so as to aid in securing prompt communications . Every possible precaution has been taken in view of the difficulties of the weather and the season to insure safety and success J. W Garrett
